{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_kesetlg3vtayuf6s4bujbzauf6hnz3sae6sk44rt72dxz6aqjhja","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_014dasLvo8UBKZWj6oityHJc","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"# Now let's verify what happens with pop on empty stack in C\n# In Rust we have unwrap_or(0). In C the underlying memory might be uninitialized\n# but on actual run, sstack_new allocates a single node uninitialized.\n# So spop would access top->data which might be garbage. Let's not test that since\n# behavior between C and Rust differ. Let's focus on well-defined ops.\n\n# Test that exit returns top of stack correctly\n./run_bytes 0x01 7 0x01 5 0x02 0x00 2>&1\n# 7 + 5 = 12\n\n# Goto behavior: PUSH 1, GOTO 6, EXIT, PUSH 99, EXIT\n# bytes: 1, 1, 9, 6, 0x00, 0x01, 99, 0x00 -> at pc=2 GOTO, pc=3 read 6, pop 1 == 1, so pc=6, then exit, stack empty -> 0\n# Actually let's set stack so EXIT returns something\n# program: PUSH 1, PUSH 1, GOTO 7, PUSH 99, EXIT, PUSH 5, EXIT\n# bytes:  0x01 1 0x01 1 0x09 7 0x01 99 0x00 0x01 5 0x00\n# pc=0: PUSH 1, pc=2\n# pc=2: PUSH 1, pc=4\n# pc=4: GOTO, pc=5, top=1, pc=p[5]=7\n# pc=7: PUSH 99, pc=9\n# pc=9: ?? check codes\n# Let's just hand-craft: PUSH 1, PUSH 1, GOTO @ pc=4, target=8\n./run_bytes 0x01 1 0x01 1 0x09 8 0x01 50 0x00 0x01 99 0x00 2>&1\n# pc=4 GOTO, pc=5 target=8, pop 1 (one of stacked 1s), pc=8 -> EXIT. Stack now has [1] -> returns 1\n# Wait stack push 1 then 1, then GOTO pops top 1, so stack=[1], at pc=8 EXIT pops 1 -> returns 1","description":"Test goto behavior"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":455,"cache_read_input_tokens":49106,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":455,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":23,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2dd3c7a2-9620-4768-9fed-8d1586371d30","uuid":"20207970-fe